docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:1:# ADR 0015: Safe Parallel Task Branch Integration
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:9:When `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` is greater than `1`, multiple Task Branches can start from the same Loop-Start Branch tip. After the first completed Task Branch fast-forwards the Loop-Start Branch, another unrelated Task Branch from the original tip is no longer a direct fast-forward even when its file changes do not conflict.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:11:Treating that non-fast-forward shape as merge attention blocks useful parallel work. Rebasing the Task Branch would require force-pushing if Stage Push already published it. Creating a direct merge commit on the Loop-Start Branch would change the current model where the Loop-Start Branch moves only by fast-forwarding to a Task Branch tip.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:15:Automated Task Branch Integration is serialized and keeps the final Loop-Start Branch update fast-forward only.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:17:Stage Commit still runs before Stage Push. Stage Push, when enabled, remains non-force and runs before Task Branch Integration.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:19:If the Loop-Start Branch is a Protected Trunk Branch, Symphony does not run automated Task Branch Integration.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:21:If the Loop-Start Branch already contains the Task Branch, Symphony treats the task as already integrated and applies eligible Task Cleanup Policy.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:23:If the Loop-Start Branch can fast-forward directly to the completed Task Branch, Symphony preserves the direct fast-forward path.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:25:If direct fast-forward is not possible, Symphony merges the current Loop-Start Branch into the completed Task Branch from the Agent Worktree. When that update succeeds, Symphony fast-forwards the Loop-Start Branch to the updated Task Branch tip. The integration merge commit belongs to the Task Branch and exists only to connect the current Loop-Start Branch history with the completed task work.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:27:If the Task Branch update conflicts or the updated Task Branch still cannot fast-forward the Loop-Start Branch, Symphony moves the task to the Merge Attention Status and keeps the Agent Worktree for inspection.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:29:Startup Reconciliation uses the same Task Branch Integration rules as normal completion. Manual Task Merge keeps its explicit operator-selected fast-forward-only semantics.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:31:Runtime State records Task Branch Integration diagnostics so operators can distinguish already-contained work, direct fast-forward, update-then-fast-forward, and attention outcomes.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:35:Unrelated concurrently completed Task Branches can integrate into the Loop-Start Branch without avoidable merge attention.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:37:Published Task Branches do not need force-push because integration updates are ordinary merge commits on the Task Branch.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:41:True conflicts remain visible as human attention, with the Agent Worktree retained for inspection.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:43:Batch Pull Request behavior remains based on work already integrated into the Loop-Start Branch and remains blocked while Merge Attention Status or unresolved orchestration attention exists.
docs/adr/0013-self-dogfooding-through-integration-branch.md:3:The Personal Symphony Product Repository can also act as a Workspace Repository for its own work, but allowing automated Task Branch integration directly into `main` would bypass normal Product Repository review and release safeguards. Self-dogfooding runs should start from a dedicated non-trunk Loop-Start Branch, such as `symphony/dogfood`, fast-forward merge completed Task Branches into that branch, and open a Batch Pull Request to `main` only after Orchestration Idle.
docs/adr/0016-issue-comments-in-agent-context.md:1:# ADR 0016: Issue Comments in Agent Context
docs/adr/0016-issue-comments-in-agent-context.md:8:when Stage Goal Handoff is enabled.
docs/adr/0016-issue-comments-in-agent-context.md:10:Issue comments supplement the Agent Prompt and Stage Goal Context. They do not replace the issue
docs/adr/0016-issue-comments-in-agent-context.md:11:description, Stage Agent instructions, Stage Skill Load, or Runtime Contract prompt template.
docs/adr/0016-stage-concurrency-policy.md:9:`agent.maxConcurrentAgents` is a global orchestration limit. It prevents Symphony from launching more than the configured total number of agents, but it does not let a Workspace Repository reserve or cap work by Stage Agent.
docs/adr/0016-stage-concurrency-policy.md:15:Runtime Settings may define a Stage Concurrency Policy on each configured Stage Agent mapping.
docs/adr/0016-stage-concurrency-policy.md:24:Stage capacity is counted against the Stage Agent mapping selected at launch time. It is not inferred only from the issue's current tracker status, because `startStatus` can move an issue before the child agent exits.
docs/adr/0016-stage-concurrency-policy.md:32:Workspace Repositories can shape concurrent work by Stage Agent while keeping the global agent limit as the hard ceiling.
docs/adr/0016-stage-concurrency-policy.md:34:Existing Runtime Settings continue to behave as they do today when no per-stage `maxConcurrentAgents` values are present.
docs/adr/0016-stage-concurrency-policy.md:40:This decision does not change Stage Commit, Stage Push, retry, Task Branch Integration, Task Cleanup Policy, or Batch Pull Request behavior.
docs/adr/0019-agent-context-snapshot-runtime-semantics.md:1:# Generate Agent Context Snapshot in the Symphony launch harness
docs/adr/0019-agent-context-snapshot-runtime-semantics.md:3:Personal Symphony needs bounded launch context for agents, but global Codex lifecycle hooks would make Workspace Repository behavior depend on user-level Codex configuration outside the Runtime Contract. Symphony will generate Agent Context Snapshot content in its own launch harness, scoped to the active Workspace Repository, and will not install or mutate global Codex hooks.
docs/adr/0019-agent-context-snapshot-runtime-semantics.md:5:Runtime Settings own operator-configurable context behavior, Agent Prompt composition owns prompt injection, Runtime State may expose live generation status, and ignored Runtime Diagnostics may store bounded secret-free metadata. Agent Context Snapshot supplements the Agent Prompt and coexists with Stage Goal Handoff; it does not replace either. Context Command stdout may supplement the Agent Context Snapshot, while stderr and command failures remain diagnostic by default.
docs/adr/0019-batch-pull-request-self-target-readiness.md:3:When automatic Batch Pull Request creation is enabled, Symphony opens one pull request from the Loop-Start Branch to the configured Pull Request Base Branch. GitHub rejects a pull request whose head and base are the same branch, and letting that fail at handoff time leaves completed work with a retryable-looking error that operator action cannot fix by retrying.
docs/adr/0019-batch-pull-request-self-target-readiness.md:5:Symphony now reports a Readiness Gap when the current Loop-Start Branch equals `pullRequest.baseBranch`. Operators must either switch to a non-trunk Loop-Start Branch before starting orchestration or change the Pull Request Base Branch to the intended target.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:19:Richer commit metadata should be modeled as Stage Commit Classification in Runtime Settings, with
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:23:four-character tag. Runtime Settings own the classification taxonomy, stage defaults, label mappings,
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:38:authoritative classification metadata unless a future Runtime Settings contract explicitly enables
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:42:Stage Agent. Symphony renders configured skills into the Agent Prompt with the normal Stage Skill
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:44:does not create the Stage Commit and does not replace Runtime Settings classification.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:51:Runtime Settings remain the source of truth for Stage Commit messages.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:56:Stage Push, Task Branch Integration, auto-merge, Manual Task Merge, and Batch Pull Request behavior
docs/adr/0016-protected-path-policy.md:15:Runtime Settings will define the first version of the Protected Path Policy. The policy will not introduce a standalone `.symphonyignore` file in the first version.
docs/adr/0016-protected-path-policy.md:23:Startup Reconciliation, automated Task Branch Integration, Manual Task Merge, and Batch Pull Request handoff must also respect unresolved protected-path attention. Startup Reconciliation and Manual Task Merge must inspect committed Task Branch work before integration. Batch Pull Request creation remains blocked while protected-path attention is unresolved.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:1:# Allowed Loop-Start Branch Policy
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:9:Protected Trunk Branches prevent automated Task Branch integration into configured trunks, but they do not stop Symphony from starting orchestration on an unsuitable Loop-Start Branch. A Self-Dogfooding Workspace Repository needs a readiness gate so automated dogfood dispatch starts from a dedicated integration branch instead of from the Product Repository trunk.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:13:Runtime Settings may define `git.allowedLoopStartBranches` as the Allowed Loop-Start Branch Policy. The first version matches literal local branch names only.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:15:When `git.allowedLoopStartBranches` is omitted or an empty list, Symphony allows any Loop-Start Branch to preserve existing Runtime Contract behavior and Bootstrap defaults. When the list is non-empty, Symphony compares the current named Loop-Start Branch to the configured set during readiness validation. If the branch is not allowed, or the checkout has no named branch, Symphony reports a Readiness Gap with the current branch state, allowed branch names, and the remediation to switch branches or update Runtime Settings.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:17:The policy is separate from `protectedTrunkBranches`. Protected Trunk Branches still control automated Task Branch integration. The Allowed Loop-Start Branch Policy controls whether automated orchestration may start at all.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:21:A disallowed Loop-Start Branch blocks dispatch before creating Agent Worktrees, moving tracker statuses, running Startup Reconciliation integration, or opening a Batch Pull Request. The Terminal Console and Web Dashboard remain available because Readiness Gaps are served as Runtime State.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:9:Safe parallel Task Branch Integration can update a completed Task Branch by merging the current Loop-Start Branch into the Agent Worktree, then fast-forwarding the Loop-Start Branch to the updated Task Branch. When that Task Branch update conflicts, Symphony previously stopped immediately in the Merge Attention Status.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:11:That behavior is conservative, but it leaves resolvable conflicts for the operator even though the Agent Worktree already contains the exact conflicted state an agent can inspect and repair.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:15:Runtime Settings may enable a Git Policy `conflictResolution` block with `enabled` and `maxAttempts`.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:17:When auto-merge fails while updating the Task Branch from the Loop-Start Branch, and Merge Conflict Resolution Handoff is enabled, Symphony launches Codex inside the conflicted Agent Worktree. The handoff prompt tells the agent to resolve conflicts, preserve both task and Loop-Start Branch intent, stage the resolved files, and create the merge commit on the Task Branch.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:19:After the conflict-resolution agent exits successfully, Symphony requires the Agent Worktree to be clean. It then fast-forwards the Loop-Start Branch to the resolved Task Branch. If the Agent Worktree remains dirty, the agent fails, the attempt limit is reached, or the resolved Task Branch cannot fast-forward the Loop-Start Branch, Symphony moves the task to the Merge Attention Status and keeps the Agent Worktree for inspection.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:25:Operators can opt into agent-assisted conflict repair without changing the invariant that the final Loop-Start Branch update is fast-forward only.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:27:The conflict-resolution merge commit belongs to the Task Branch, so published Task Branches still do not need force-push.
docs/adr/0017-merge-conflict-resolution-handoff.md:29:True or unresolved conflicts remain visible as human attention, with the Agent Worktree retained.
